  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1) \n\n--> Updated to issue a Flood Watch across southeast portions of the \n area. \n\nMid level short wave energy will drop down across the mid and upper\nOhio Valley today, followed by a cold front that will push southeast\nacross our area tonight. There are currently two areas of ongoing \nclusters of storms associated with this, one up across northern \nlower Michigan and another across Iowa and portions of northern \nIllinois. Most of the hi-res models are suggesting some additional \ndevelopment through the pre-dawn hours along the northeast side of \nthe cluster back across Iowa/Illinois, with this new activity then \ndropping down into our area from the northwest toward daybreak. \n\nThis initial activity will likely start off as elevated, but as we \nprogress through mid to late morning, low level moisture/theta e \nadvection up into our area will allow for some surface based \ninstability to possibly develop by late morning. If this all comes \ntogether, there could be a low end severe risk across mainly our \nsouthwestern areas with this first round of storms. \n\nThis first round of storms will also lead to some uncertainty as to \nhow well we will recover across the area as we head into this\nafternoon. Again though, given the continued theta e advection into \nour area, we should see at least some recovery through the afternoon\nhours with sbcapes possibly recovering into the 1000-2000 J/KG range.\nIf this occurs, we should see additional redevelopment through the \nafternoon hours, possibly focusing on remnant boundaries form this \nmorning's convection. The best chance for this would likely be for \nareas along and southeast of the I-71 corridor. Finally, some of the\nhi-res models are trying to show some redevelopment along/ahead of \nthe font late this afternoon into this evening. \n\nAll of this makes for a somewhat conditional and not totally clear \nsevere threat for today into this evening. However, given the strong\nwind fields (0-6km and 0-3km shears both in the 40-50 knot range), \nsome severe storms will be possible during the afternoon and early \nevening. Again the best chance for this appear to be along and\nsoutheast of the I-71 corridor. While damaging wind from bowing \nsegments will be the primary threat, some large hail and an isolated\ntornado cannot be ruled out if some of the storms are able to \nsustain better rotation, mostly likely earlier in the event this \nafternoon. \n\nPWs, will again be in excess of two inches so heavy downpours can be\nexpected. The activity does look to be somewhat progressive though \nso this should help limit a more widespread flash flood threat. That \nbeing said, some localized flash flooding will be possible this \nafternoon and evening, especially across our far southeastern areas,\nwhere thunderstorms coverage is expected to be maximized.\n\nKey Message 2) \n\nSurface high pressure will build into the region behind the \nfront for mid week. This will usher in a cooler airmass with highs \ntemperatures only in the 70s for Wednesday and Thursday. Temperatures \nwill then begin to slowly moderate as we head into the weekend.",
